findings.verification_note:
I traced the code path and it matches the finding. agent.py:131-137 `build_tool_prompt` only emits `- {name}: {description}`, and config/system_prompt.py names arguments only for edit_file. The descriptions in tools/registry.py give no argument names for get_news (line 40, "Get latest news headlines"), search_web (line 46) or read_web_content (line 52, "(list of URLs)"). They also don't say "Takes no arguments" for get_project_structure (line 4) or read_calendar (line 63). verify_app is the only tool that says it. The signatures are strict: tools/web.py:59 `get_news(country)`, :44 `search_web(query)`, :20 `read_web_content(urls)`, tools/files.py:41 `get_project_structure()`, features/calendar/calendar.py:186 `read_calendar()`. Unlike read_files, search_files and write_file, none of them accepts **kwargs or aliases. agent.py:128 `execute_tool` calls `func(**args)` with the model's dict unchanged. The call at agent.py:309 is outside any try, so the TypeError escapes `_run_steps`. Effects: it kills the CLI loop (`__main__` has no try), it cuts off the `/ask_stream` NDJSON stream in the middle (core/agent_routes.py:191-193 has no handler), and in `/ask` it only shows up as a generic error.

I reproduced it read-only with `agent.execute_tool`. Calling it with these arguments raised TypeError in every case:
- get_news {'topic':'tech'}
- get_news {}: "missing 1 required positional argument: 'country'"
- search_web {'q':..}
- read_web_content {'url':..}
- get_project_structure {'path':'.'}
- read_calendar {'date':..}

No network call happens because argument binding fails before the function body runs. No test covers mismatched argument names for these tools. tests/test_agent_flow.py only uses the correct `query` and `{}`.

One nuance, which doesn't change the finding: the logged tool_execution entries show the model using `query`/`urls` correctly and `{}` for the zero-argument tools. Most of those entries look like test fixtures, though ("largest mountain", "http://a"). Failed calls are never logged, because log_event runs after execute_tool, so the log can't show how often this happens. The most likely real trigger is get_news, because its required `country` parameter isn't obvious from the description. Medium severity is reasonable.
